#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161842 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "f4a3741e-a87c-5b57-95b3-5ef47f7ad599" :question/math-academy-id "e-9907" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are $7, 2,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $7,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 7\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\}  = \\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161843 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "06ca6e76-6354-5450-882e-b0c063acd303" :question/math-academy-id "e-9908" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/094b6043b9cce7f3ac2a7cc32fa53785d3fbf5b490ce853474f89bb4afa8291a.png)\n\nA $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA,$ find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1,$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_1|| = 8 = \\sigma_1,$ which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64,$ and\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_2|| = 6 = \\sigma_2,$ which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = 36$ of $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/id #uuid "23c1671b-da2c-570b-af4f-c6aad258155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9909" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4,$ $\\sigma_2=2,$ and $\\sigma_3 = 0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4,$ the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91474655-212b-55f0-b086-d5578984f699" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159259} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b90e3d-bf69-5b8c-9214-a72ea025c69f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159260} {:answer/id #uuid "08277399-e698-5fa3-b0f6-be9ce2be8dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159261} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3eff44-c5d5-58e0-bce9-ce8d2b9b322c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159262} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0777fb-f320-5948-85f4-e21a9e448196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08277399-e698-5fa3-b0f6-be9ce2be8dd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159261} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4fc5061-2971-509d-ae61-dd733bd377be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2e59ffd-322c-5739-bff6-4abbfbcffef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139415" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $5,$ $3,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $5,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=5\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e082731-db2e-5189-a6e9-b8b1ddaa147f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159265} {:answer/id #uuid "e4076dfa-7378-5413-949b-6eee7814eaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159266} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ad6ca8-07de-5d49-9a65-e5f5986dda2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159267} {:answer/id #uuid "210eafc0-0495-5eda-8a5e-427af20603f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159268} {:answer/id #uuid "609d0378-debd-59bb-97e4-a31ee887e6f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "210eafc0-0495-5eda-8a5e-427af20603f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159268} :answer-field/id #uuid "46d46c35-336b-5f41-82ca-8de59de1a00c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cdd91aaf-b8dd-5936-93a2-ee3b24ba20f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139418" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={10}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={10}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87b494d3-0b61-5e52-9f72-23c3476b198e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159277} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6ee90f-366c-5f5b-b1a2-f07695f27dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7v_{1} + 5v_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159278} {:answer/id #uuid "94d74135-f328-5641-b2bb-0bbd125b85a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159279} {:answer/id #uuid "dfba7e32-6af6-541d-98d3-95ad4d788b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159280} {:answer/id #uuid "165ed7a9-687f-5aef-ade0-11c4b7c0989a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94d74135-f328-5641-b2bb-0bbd125b85a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159279} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30b56ee-52cf-5e42-ab2b-2bbf82995677" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14414eae-18ed-5455-a3d4-1f9724606970" :question/math-academy-id "q-139421" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/f26e876d5234313926a2cbaa8938e32c8a725cf3435ce3113361bfa3dc1b7997.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={7}^{2}=49,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={5}^{2}=25.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=25$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b713ed-8481-5ccf-bca4-3e6caac54d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384451} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ea1eb0-b6a6-518f-98d2-d3aa6475a260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384452} {:answer/id #uuid "f06e827e-945a-5dc5-a1e9-46ceebeff7ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384453} {:answer/id #uuid "eea04fa8-1db9-56b5-a65c-82f8b9814b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384454} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9d027e-8b3a-5ced-aa30-652441416dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10b713ed-8481-5ccf-bca4-3e6caac54d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384451} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc60fbd-7f74-5a3a-9230-dd4b7793d7a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d8fbb39-d61e-55b7-943c-0294b7ca4f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-139492" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=12\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144ab559-a16e-58c3-a1ca-cbef661ac560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384458} {:answer/id #uuid "85f2c2f5-f272-586e-80f3-afca1fe8fc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384459} {:answer/id #uuid "2b778047-abf5-502c-b49d-aa23a595ea5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384460} {:answer/id #uuid "43c78f17-d774-5a50-95ac-7a0e0e82d14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384461} {:answer/id #uuid "9bab4bb6-efef-53c2-8bd2-a4ca9aa1994d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43c78f17-d774-5a50-95ac-7a0e0e82d14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384461} :answer-field/id #uuid "9efeaf49-593c-5847-b029-5a818aebc382" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e6f8d45-04e8-5636-bfad-79a24d0d0d39" :question/math-academy-id "q-139526" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $15,$ $10,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $15,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=15\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{15}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e12e8a9-40ad-5aec-8813-60881c2cd5e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384465} {:answer/id #uuid "aab37fa0-a681-503b-8060-ccd54b024c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384466} {:answer/id #uuid "36408f96-367b-588e-90b6-4649a467c3f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384467} {:answer/id #uui