\\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $9,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}=9\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df5cbe66-44b4-57fe-8ac6-fe7370138f4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384479} {:answer/id #uuid "37db1ace-7c2a-521c-84d6-5535e035437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384480} {:answer/id #uuid "23f4b3da-60e0-53cf-9387-2384249e21fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384481} {:answer/id #uuid "250b8c4e-3b8f-55c0-8b27-8af2310d14e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384482} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ed9fb-3c7e-52a5-bc81-ec7a5ff969d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23f4b3da-60e0-53cf-9387-2384249e21fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384481} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4a9d64f-115a-5bbe-ad05-955f63449233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7e0db86-a364-51b2-8a45-6b2ec69cd12f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139535" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={9}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={9}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=9.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "524e30eb-87c6-5cfc-a4a2-a7116a462d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384486} {:answer/id #uuid "423b097e-f5ad-5a52-bdb0-0076f40e34e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/val