^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={5}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={5}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf27e955-daa2-5963-8445-2733ab3aa59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384493} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4c3f77-0571-5e06-abe3-c851feb5ab36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384494} {:answer/id #uuid "74dbd5ed-5fbb-5bd0-b0de-061dee28f5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384495} {:answer/id #uuid "f8fc340d-8bc9-5862-b7bd-f9a5d4eb6f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384496} {:answer/id #uuid "80bf5c3b-5602-5112-8ea9-b9322798f630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8fc340d-8bc9-5862-b7bd-f9a5d4eb6f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384496} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c15ba5f-12a3-5e39-ad44-3d0de6d92224" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60986dd5-ba5e-5ae4-9f24-afea4641cac1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139542" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1297d770-3b01-5ac0-b2cf-890c96948308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384500} {:answer/id #uuid "8928aa31-5aee-5bfd-b2ec-68b06f2e556b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384501} {:answer/id #uuid "6be5b243-7d13-59f8-83f0-a416580973ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384502} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfc4488-b264-5ebf-a17b-3e4664eb0fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384503} {:answer/id #uuid "bf54994c-45e0-59e8-8d36-87ab82b3479d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1297d770-3b01-5ac0-b2cf-890c96948308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384500} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0234460-3aa7-5565-9143-51565167c5eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3dd19228-551e-52ff-9794-4eaf046f7c19" :question/math-academy-id "q-139543" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=9,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={12}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={12}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d1d7ee4-9447-5f7d-a4d3-7def6e537ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384507} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9dcb70-4f65-5f81-919d-34b7e37de744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8{v}_{1}$ and $5{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384508} {:answer/id #uuid "249782b9-0e40-54ae-8c5f-6a6e18117c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384509} {:answer/id #uuid "041c8409-90ca-5960-bb66-d08f274d248d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$16$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384510} {:answer/id #uuid "42d8d06f-45f5-5a34-a3ec-16696eb1b0bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42d8d06f-45f5-5a34-a3ec-16696eb1b0bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384511} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcba808c-3c33-5777-8c48-ea8fd82b3cfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0a080ae-086d-5da1-af59-3299d460a5c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139547" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14077831/assets/d4947e37239d2ac2950b536d7afc2d0810ea713c437b7095a43e123d50ff2b34.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4a0a15d-5c4e-5c72-84b4-dd289af78a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7{v}_{1}$ and $5{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384514} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7ed6c9-f312-502d-8873-97cbfe81ea1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384515} {:answer/id #uuid "93363083-3d10-5eec-858d-a7b261cb04a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384516} {:answer/id #uuid "0c802013-3109-5794-86d7-d89ff11a04fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.t